                "markdown": "The description you provided does not seem to be related to a specific C++ vulnerability. The URL you provided is a link to Stack Overflow, a popular programming Q&A site, and does not contain any C++ code to analyze for vulnerabilities.\n\nHowever, I can provide a general explanation of what an \"Unsecured URL Vulnerability\" might refer to. This is not specific to C++, but rather a general web security issue. An unsecured URL vulnerability typically refers to a situation where sensitive data is transmitted over an unsecured (non-HTTPS) connection, or where the URL itself contains sensitive information (like a password or session ID) that could be intercepted or logged.\n\nMitigation advice for this kind of vulnerability would typically involve:\n\n- Always using HTTPS for any connections that transmit sensitive data.\n- Never including sensitive data directly in URLs.\n- Using secure methods for managing and transmitting session information, such as secure cookies.\n\nSince this isn't a C++ specific issue, there aren't any C++ library dependencies to list, and there isn't a specific piece of C++ code to fix.\n\nFor more information on secure coding practices, you might find the following OWASP resources helpful:\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [OWASP Top 10](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\nThe closest matching CWE for this kind of issue might be [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)."
